WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.560
In the first film, one ventricular cell held its plateau.

2
00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:09.200
Now nearly every ventricular cell is up there together, far above rest.

3
00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:12.140
Yet the tracing on the skin is flat.

4
00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:14.460
Why?

5
00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:22.303
A natural guess says the ECG is all the action potentials added up.

6
00:00:23.463 --> 00:00:25.723
But that sum would stay high right through the plateau.

7
00:00:26.917 --> 00:00:31.437
An electrode on the chest cannot record any one cell's action potential.

8
00:00:32.657 --> 00:00:35.337
What reaches it is current in the fluid around the cells.

9
00:00:36.577 --> 00:00:39.957
That current flows only where neighboring cells differ in voltage.

10
00:00:41.117 --> 00:00:44.837
Picture a strip of muscle with a wave halfway along it.

11
00:00:45.897 --> 00:00:47.897
Behind the wave, cells sit on their plateau.

12
00:00:49.237 --> 00:00:50.557
Ahead, they are still at rest.

13
00:00:51.567 --> 00:00:56.547
At the boundary, positive charge crosses gap junctions, and the circuit closes

14
00:00:56.547 --> 00:00:57.887
through the fluid outside.

15
00:00:59.227 --> 00:01:02.707
So the boundary acts like a small battery, travelling with the wave.

16
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:11.700
Here is the one idea.

17
00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:15.460
The ECG never shows a cell's voltage.

18
00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:21.000
It shows only differences between cells, so it sees the edges of waves.

19
00:01:26.533 --> 00:01:27.893
Now read one beat.

20
00:01:29.213 --> 00:01:32.273
At first every cell is at rest, so the trace is flat.

21
00:01:33.553 --> 00:01:36.073
Then depolarization spreads across the atria.

22
00:01:37.373 --> 00:01:38.973
That moving edge is the P wave.

23
00:01:44.467 --> 00:01:48.127
Next the wave crawls through the AV node and the bundle of His.

24
00:01:49.407 --> 00:01:50.867
An edge is still travelling.

25
00:01:52.167 --> 00:01:53.587
So should the trace move here?

26
00:01:59.483 --> 00:02:01.043
It stays nearly flat.

27
00:02:02.343 --> 00:02:06.163
Those structures are too small for their boundary to register at the skin.

28
00:02:07.223 --> 00:02:10.563
The more tissue a boundary spans, the bigger the deflection.

29
00:02:11.717 --> 00:02:16.177
Then the wave races through the Purkinje network and crosses the ventricles.

30
00:02:17.377 --> 00:02:20.297
That large moving edge is the QRS complex.

31
00:02:21.697 --> 00:02:23.357
Its width is the crossing time.

32
00:02:28.817 --> 00:02:32.217
Now nearly every ventricular cell is on its plateau.

33
00:02:33.517 --> 00:02:37.697
With no edge left to travel, the ST segment is flat, or nearly so.

34
00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:45.130
Recovery makes one more edge.

35
00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:51.150
Different regions repolarize at different times, which forms a broad, slow boundary.

36
00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:53.910
That boundary is the T wave.

37
00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:06.940
The QT interval runs from the start of QRS to the end of T. A longer plateau means a

38
00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:07.940
longer QT.

39
00:03:08.833 --> 00:03:11.433
So the skin learns little from a heart in agreement.

40
00:03:12.713 --> 00:03:15.933
All at rest, or all on the plateau, the line stays level.

41
00:03:17.273 --> 00:03:19.333
Only moving disagreement writes the beat.
